    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe mapping works and sits in the right file, but it breaks this mapper's established convention for how derived fields with branching/null logic are expressed.\n\n**Finding: inline a null-guarded ternary where the file's convention is a `default` method.**\n\nLook at the two groups of `@Mapping` expressions in this file:\n\n- Straight-line, branchless expressions stay inline: `displayName` (concat), `initials` (substring+concat), `bulkSignupWarning`/`possibleDuplicate` (`Boolean.TRUE.equals(...)`).\n- Every mapping that needs a conditional / null-guard is extracted to a `default` method: `salutation`, `contactPreference`, `ageBand`, `membershipNumber`, `fiscalYear`, `checkDigit`, `locality`, `timezone` \u2014 several of which open with exactly the `if (x == null) return null;` shape.\n\n`selfLink` is the only inline expression in the file carrying an explicit `owner.getId() == null ? null : ...` branch, plus a hardcoded `\"/api/owners/\"` path literal buried in an annotation string. That is precisely the kind of logic this mapper otherwise lifts out. A maintainer scanning the file would expect it as:\n\n```java\n@Mapping(target = \"selfLink\", expression = \"java(selfLink(owner))\")\n...\n/**\n * The canonical API path to this owner, {@code /api/owners/<id>}. Null until the\n * owner has been assigned an id.\n */\ndefault String selfLink(Owner owner) {\n    return owner.getId() == null ? null : \"/api/owners/\" + owner.getId();\n}\n```\n\nThis keeps the branch/null-guard and the path literal in a readable, testable unit consistent with the neighbouring `salutation`/`contactPreference` methods, rather than as the lone branching one-liner embedded in the annotation. (The `OwnerTelephones`/`OwnerIdentity` helper-class route is also available, but a `default` method matches the closest existing siblings and avoids creating a class for a one-liner.)\n\nThe OpenAPI `selfLink` schema addition is fine and well-placed.",
